<h1>Automatic Tray Loading &amp; Unloading Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An automatic tray loader places product into trays without hand-packing, and an automatic tray unloader does the reverse: it removes product from trays or separates stacked trays for reuse. A loading cell combines a tray denester, product infeed conveyor, placement mechanism (cam-driven pick-and-place, servo gantry, or delta robot), and outfeed conveyor. Placement method is set by throughput, SKU count, and product fragility, all compared below.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Automate Tray Loading?</h2>
<p>Manual tray loading remains one of the most labor-intensive tasks on a packaging line. A manual tray loading station often needs several operators per shift, so on multi-shift lines the labor case for an automatic tray loader is usually the first justification. Payback depends on local labor cost, shift pattern, and line uptime, so calculate it for your own line rather than relying on a generic figure.</p>
<p>Beyond labor savings, automatic tray loading delivers three operational advantages. First, <strong>consistency</strong>: machines place every product in the same orientation, at the same position, every cycle. Manual loading introduces variation that causes downstream problems at lidding, sealing, and case-packing stations. Second, <strong>speed</strong>: a machine holds its rated rate for the full shift, while manual crews slow with fatigue and turnover. Third, <strong>hygiene</strong>: reducing human contact with product is critical in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing environments</a> where contamination risk drives recalls.</p>
<p>The decision to automate tray loading usually begins when manual crews cannot keep up with line rate or when the product requires precise orientation for downstream sealing or labeling. At lower rates, semi-automatic systems with operator-assisted placement often provide a cost-effective middle ground.</p>
<h2 id="tech-heading">Tray Loading Technologies</h2>
<h3>Cam-Driven Pick-and-Place</h3>
<p>Cam-driven pick-and-place systems use mechanical cams to create repeatable, high-speed motion profiles. The pick head follows a fixed oval or elliptical path, down to pick, up and over, down to place. Because the motion is fixed, cam-driven systems are typically the fastest option for small, uniform products like cookies, crackers, and confectionery pieces.</p>
<p>The trade-off is flexibility. Cam-driven systems excel at one specific motion profile. Changing the pick pattern, tray size, or product dimensions often requires mechanical changeover, swapping cams, adjusting stroke lengths, and re-timing the infeed. Lines running a single SKU at high volume benefit most from this technology.</p>
<h3>Servo Gantry Systems</h3>
<p>Servo gantry tray loaders replace mechanical cams with servo motors on X, Y, and Z axes. The motion profile is programmable, operators change pick positions, place positions, and motion paths through the HMI without mechanical adjustment. Speed depends on travel distance and product weight, and is generally below cam-driven systems on the same product.</p>
<p>Servo gantry systems accept a wider range of tray sizes and product configurations than cam-driven units. Changeover involves loading a new recipe on the HMI and, in some cases, swapping the end-of-arm tooling (vacuum cups, grippers, or suction plates). Because changeover is mostly software, servo gantry is a common choice for lines running a moderate number of SKUs.</p>
<p>The servo drive also enables soft-start and soft-stop profiles that protect fragile products, decorated cakes, frosted pastries, and delicate produce, from acceleration damage. Variable speed profiles let the system slow down during the pick phase and accelerate during the traverse phase, optimizing both gentleness and throughput.</p>
<h3>Delta Robot Tray Loading</h3>
<p>Delta robots (also called spider robots or parallel kinematic robots) mount above the conveyor and use three or four arms connected to a common platform. Vision-guided delta robots track product on a moving conveyor belt, compute pick coordinates in real time, and place product into trays. Manufacturer ratings for light products run from about 120 to 300 picks per minute per robot: <a href="https://new.abb.com/news/detail/93173/prsrl-ABB-launches-five-axis-Delta-robot-fastest-for-lightweight-product-picking-packing-and-re-orientation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ABB rates its IRB 365</a> at 120 picks per minute with a 1 kg product, and rates the <a href="https://www.industrialtechnology.co.uk/omron-debuts-worlds-fastest-and-first-4-arm-delta-robot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Omron Quattro</a> at up to 300. Real tray loading rates are lower once vision, product weight, and tray pattern are included.</p>
<p>The key advantage of delta robots is random-infeed capability. Product arrives on the infeed conveyor in no particular order or spacing. The vision system photographs the belt, identifies each product's position and orientation, and assigns picks to the robot in real time. This eliminates the need for upstream singulation, orientation, and precise spacing, functions that require significant conveyor length and complexity with non-robotic systems.</p>
<p>Multi-robot cells place two to six delta robots in series over a single conveyor. Each robot picks product that upstream robots missed. This redundancy means the cell continues running at reduced capacity if one robot goes down for maintenance. As a scale reference, <a href="https://www.packworld.com/leaders-new/machinery/conveying-accumulation/article/13325677/robotic-tray-loaders-speed-cookie-loading" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Packaging World reported</a> a Voortman cookie line using 10 vision-guided robots to load five trays at 1,100 cookies per minute. BluePrint Automation's Vision Guided Robotics (VGR) systems and ABB's FlexPicker IRB 360 are widely used in bakery and snack food tray loading.</p>
<h3>Tray Denesting &amp; Feeding</h3>
<p>Every automatic tray loading cell requires a tray denester, the mechanism that separates individual trays from a nested stack and places them on the loading conveyor. Denesting reliability is critical because a tray-feed jam stops the entire cell. Common denesting mechanisms include vacuum cup separators, mechanical finger separators, and air-knife assisted systems.</p>
<p>Vacuum cup denesters grip the top tray with suction cups and pull it from the stack. They work well with rigid thermoformed trays (CPET, APET, polypropylene) but struggle with thin, flexible trays that deform under vacuum. Mechanical finger denesters use spring-loaded fingers to separate trays by inserting into the nesting flange. They handle a broader range of tray materials but require adjustment when tray flange dimensions change.</p>
<p>Stack capacity matters for operator ergonomics. A denester with a 200-tray magazine capacity reduces reload frequency to once every 2–3 minutes at 80 trays/minute. Systems with automatic stack replenishment, where a new stack feeds into the magazine without stopping, eliminate reload-related downtime entirely. Shuttleworth and Benchmark Automation offer continuous-feed denesting options for high-speed lines.</p>
<h2 id="conveyors-heading">Infeed &amp; Outfeed Conveyors</h2>
<p>Tray loading systems depend on two critical conveyor sections: the product infeed and the tray outfeed. The infeed conveyor delivers product to the pick zone in the correct orientation, spacing, and speed. The outfeed conveyor carries loaded trays to the next process, typically lidding, sealing, over-wrapping, or case packing.</p>
<h3>Product Infeed Conveyor</h3>
<p>The product infeed conveyor must deliver product to the pick zone at a consistent, controlled rate. For cam-driven and servo gantry systems, the infeed typically uses an <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> that positions each product at a precise pick point. For delta robot systems, the infeed uses a continuously running belt conveyor with a vision camera mounted upstream, the robot calculates pick coordinates from the vision data and tracks the moving product.</p>
<p>Infeed conveyor belt material matters for food applications. White FDA-approved polyurethane belting is standard for direct food contact. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary conveyor designs</a> with tool-free belt removal, open-frame construction, and IP69K-rated motors simplify daily washdown. Dorner's AquaPruf series and Shuttleworth's slip-torque conveyors are popular infeed choices for bakery and snack applications.</p>
<h3>Tray Outfeed Conveyor</h3>
<p>The tray outfeed conveyor carries loaded trays from the loading station to downstream processes. This conveyor must handle the combined weight of the tray plus product without tipping or shifting the load. For lightweight trays (berries, cookies), flat belt conveyors work well. For heavier trays (ready meals, meat), <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">powered roller conveyors</a> or <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">tabletop chain conveyors</a> provide more stability.</p>
<p>Accumulation on the outfeed side is critical. Downstream machines (sealers, lidders) often cycle slower than the tray loader. Without <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a> capacity between the loader and the sealer, the tray loader must stop every time the sealer cycles, reducing effective throughput by 15–30%. Zero-pressure accumulation using <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> is the preferred solution for tray outfeed buffering.</p>
